WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
And ai just turned one short description into a full playable game about something huge and strange arriving in a quiet suburb. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 196. Argon is the name of that artificial intelligence system, shared in a post by Ethan Mollick on X. The written request it received was about a game called Imminence. The exact wording asked for something very big and very strange happening. It wanted a suburb and the arrival of a vast and unknowable presence. It said not horror and not sad or sad.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:30.320
scary.

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:34.180
It asks to invoke the feeling of the end of all things coming, inevitably.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:37.860
Players then get to live through the slow arrival of that presence.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:42.700
Hand a classmate one sentence for a movie, and they hand you a short film you can walk

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:43.480
around inside.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.480
That is closer to what Argonne is doing than a chatbot that only replies with paragraphs.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:51.720
It does not stop at writing words or drawing pictures.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:55.720
It assembles the whole experience so you can explore the world it created.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:01:00.540
Any one can open the finished game in a browser from the link's Ethan Mollock pointed to.

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:05.420
Game design used to mean teams of artists, programmers, and writers working for months.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:08.940
Now a single idea can become something you can actually play.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:14.400
It is a bit like Minecraft, except you describe a mood instead of placing every block by hand.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:18.900
For students and creative teens, that changes what making a game even means.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:23.460
You do not need years of coding classes to bring a strange story idea to life.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:28.560
It opens a door if you love worldbuilding, storytelling, or quiet atmospheric games,

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:31.000
but you do not have the technical skills yet.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:35.220
Your next creative project could start with a weird feeling you want to explore.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:39.360
Then the system helps turn that feeling into something you can share with classmates

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:40.420
or post online.

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:42.880
You can try a version of this right now.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:48.440
Open the playable game from Ethan Mollick's post in your browser and walk the suburb yourself.

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:53.861
After that, type your own short description of a mood or scene, and watch what kind

00:01:53.861 --> 00:01:56.261
of interactive experience appears from your words.

00:01:56.781 --> 00:02:00.481
Notice how the game stays true to the original feeling the whole time you play.

00:02:01.101 --> 00:02:05.181
Story, rules, and visuals are coming from one model working as a set.

00:02:05.701 --> 00:02:09.221
Your phone already does a baby version of this trick every time you text.

00:02:09.761 --> 00:02:12.461
It guesses the next word from everything you have already written.

00:02:12.941 --> 00:02:13.821
Stretch that guess.

00:02:14.181 --> 00:02:17.201
Instead of one word, the system guesses the entire next scene.

00:02:17.201 --> 00:02:21.781
It guesses what characters might say, and even what rule the player should follow next.

00:02:22.421 --> 00:02:28.841
First, it reads your description and splits it into small pieces, pieces like suburb, vast presence, and not scary.

00:02:29.441 --> 00:02:33.761
Then it checks those pieces against patterns from thousands of stories, games, and images.

00:02:34.381 --> 00:02:40.341
It is choosing which pieces fit the mood you described, the way a DJ picks tracks that belong on the same playlist.

00:02:41.041 --> 00:02:43.161
Next, it builds the parts you actually touch.

00:02:43.161 --> 00:02:48.201
rooms you can walk through, objects you can click, a quiet sense that something large

00:02:48.201 --> 00:02:52.341
is approaching, because it keeps predicting what should come right after the last thing

00:02:52.341 --> 00:02:57.661
it created. The world stays consistent. It does not jump from a slow piano song into

00:02:57.661 --> 00:03:02.301
a sports anthem halfway through. Okay, this one's a little tricky, but stick with me.

00:03:02.861 --> 00:03:07.241
The model does not follow a long handwritten rulebook for every possible button you might

00:03:07.241 --> 00:03:12.381
press. It simply continues the pattern of what comes next, across writing, pictures,

00:03:12.381 --> 00:03:14.481
and game logic at the same time.

00:03:14.981 --> 00:03:19.001
That steady prediction is why the finished game feels like one atmosphere rather than

00:03:19.001 --> 00:03:20.561
separate scraps glued together.

00:03:21.121 --> 00:03:25.601
When you play, every new screen or sound continues the same slow, strange feeling you

00:03:25.601 --> 00:03:26.981
asked for in the first sentence.

00:03:27.601 --> 00:03:32.061
Once you see it as autocomplete for a whole world, the mystery gets a lot smaller in your

00:03:32.061 --> 00:03:32.441
hands.

00:03:33.433 --> 00:03:37.453
In furnace AI is a new chat tool aimed at people who want more creative freedom.

00:03:38.113 --> 00:03:42.813
OpenPR reports it is built with fewer automatic blocks when you ask unusual questions.

00:03:42.813 --> 00:03:48.333
A chatbot is a program you talk to in ordinary language, like texting someone who writes back

00:03:48.333 --> 00:03:49.273
almost instantly.

00:03:49.853 --> 00:03:54.873
Mainstream tools often use safety filters, which work like a cautious hall monitor who cuts

00:03:54.873 --> 00:03:55.993
a conversation short.

00:03:56.493 --> 00:04:00.373
Inferness is designed so those talks can go further without getting shut down.

00:04:00.953 --> 00:04:05.813
If you have ever felt limited by filters on a tool like chat GPT, this is worth testing.

00:04:06.393 --> 00:04:11.173
Go to the Inferness AI site, start a free chat, and describe a wild story idea.

00:04:11.173 --> 00:04:15.993
Then ask it to continue a scene in a style you like, and notice whether the conversation

00:04:15.993 --> 00:04:16.773
keeps going.

00:04:17.273 --> 00:04:20.613
You may need a parent's help to sign up if age verification appears.

00:04:21.712 --> 00:04:25.892
A solo creator spent nearly a year making a complete game called The Last Admiral.

00:04:26.532 --> 00:04:30.772
On Reddit, they said AI tools did most of the coding, while they handled the story,

00:04:30.932 --> 00:04:32.492
the visuals, and the balance themselves.

00:04:33.232 --> 00:04:35.992
The project contains 1 million lines of code in total.

00:04:36.452 --> 00:04:39.412
400,000 of those lines are the actual game code.

00:04:39.412 --> 00:04:44.392
About 40% of that game code is comments, which are like sticky notes left in the margins

00:04:44.392 --> 00:04:49.792
so a human can still follow the work. The creator used codex and clawed to write the code and

00:04:49.792 --> 00:04:54.752
then clean it. Codex and clawed are AI helpers that produce computer instructions the way

00:04:54.752 --> 00:05:00.652
a chatbot produces sentences. They also built a special file called agents.md so the two

00:05:00.652 --> 00:05:06.312
AIs could talk to each other, like study partners passing notes. Inside a game-making workshop

00:05:06.312 --> 00:05:12.032
called the Godot engine. They built over 100 live editors. Those editors work like the setting

00:05:12.032 --> 00:05:17.232
sliders in a game, except they change visuals, audio, and mechanics without writing a fresh chunk

00:05:17.232 --> 00:05:23.512
of code each time. The last admiral has a free demo on Steam, and it will be part of Steam NextFest

00:05:23.512 --> 00:05:29.432
starting October 19th. Steam NextFest is a big online showcase where you can try upcoming games

00:05:29.432 --> 00:05:34.912
without buying them first. Head to the Steam Store, search for the last admiral, download the

00:05:34.912 --> 00:05:38.712
free demo and see what a solo creator plus AI helpers can finish.

00:05:39.581 --> 00:05:44.321
The US Office of Personnel Management launched a chatbot for federal workers, meaning

00:05:44.321 --> 00:05:46.381
people who work for the United States government.

00:05:47.081 --> 00:05:52.081
HSToday says it lets those employees quickly look up workforce data and answer common questions.

00:05:52.721 --> 00:05:56.121
It is a simple case of AI showing up in everyday government tools.

00:05:56.721 --> 00:06:01.701
A post on X collected jokes in a sparkling format where something only counts if it comes

